									</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/cybersqyd" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(5336470); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=5336470&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1606225470" alt="cybersqyd" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=5336470)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/cybersqyd" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(5336470); return false;" >cybersqyd</a></span> <span class="odate time_1606210964 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">24 Nov 2020 09:42</span>
																	<br/>
						in discussion <a href="/forum/c-89000/help:ideas-critique">Writing Help / Help: Ideas Critique</a> &raquo;
						<a href="/forum/t-13872104/double-edged">Double Edged</a>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4826823">
									

<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[+]&nbsp;Open&nbsp;for&nbsp;crit</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>However after its use something terrible of equal or greater intensity will happen that will affect the user in some way or form.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This makes this idea feel like a kinda, generic cursed magic item; like, the kinda thing you'd expect to see in a game of DnD, not a SCP. My advice would be really consider what the bad effects are here and how they can be thematically tied together: two similar uses of the sword should feel like they produce two similar outcomes; so that you can setup a feel for the reader of how this works. It might be worth considering whether you can use the effects here to imply something about the sword; like it punishing some people more than others to indicate it has like, preferences in how it's used say.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>would be found talking about its creation and its uses, such as a major example the sword being uses by a celt who used the blade to kill an entire army only for a plague to befall his village originating from the corpses that killed the same amount of people the warrior killed</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Relying on events that happened a long time ago in your narrative is generally not a great idea: it's a lot harder to get a reader invested in a battle that happened thousands of years ago to characters we can never meet with stakes that have already played out than it is to get a reader invested in a character doing something right now with an uncertain outcome.</p>
<p>At the end of the day, my broad feelings on this is that it really needs you to explore what happens now the Foundation has it: do they use it? do they test it to find out what the limits of its power are? Why does this matter?</p>
<p>Basically: at the end of the day, the PoV this story is told from is The Foundation; and in turn, you need to figure out how to get them involved in the story somehow.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

